<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Equatoria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Equatoria is a region in the far south of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a>. It was organised as a province of the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> in the 1870s, first under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-baker">Samuel Baker</a> and then Charles Gordon, to extend Egyptian rule up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a> and suppress the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. Its later governor, Emin Pasha, was cut off by the Mahdist revolt until rescued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a> in 1889. It is now divided into three states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/equatoria.jpg" alt="Equatoria" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NordNordWest · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Equatoria_in_South_Sudan_2015.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Equatoria is the southernmost region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a>, along the upper reaches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a> and the border with Uganda, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>. Juba, the national capital and largest city of South Sudan, lies within it. Its people belong to numerous ethnic groups, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zande-people">Azande</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acholi">Acholi</a>, Madi, Moru, Lokoya, Otuho and Pari, and they are traditionally peasants or nomads. Juba Arabic and English are the common languages. Equatoria now consists of the states of Central, Eastern and Western Equatoria.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> and chose the British explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-baker">Samuel Baker</a> to establish the Equatoria province, which he did in 1870. He was to set up trading posts along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a>, with Gondokoro, near present-day Juba, as an important centre. Equatorian groups such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zande-people">Azande</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a>, Lokoya, Otuho and Pari resisted outsiders, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gbudwe">Gbudwe</a> of the Azande encouraged resistance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-george-gordon">Charles George Gordon</a> governed from 1874 and founded more posts. In 1878 he was succeeded by Emin Pasha, who made his headquarters at Lado.</p><p>In 1881 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ahmad">Muhammad Ahmad</a> proclaimed himself the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, and by 1883 the Mahdists had cut off outside communications. A relief expedition under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a>, the Advance, set out in February 1887 and lost two thirds of its personnel on the way through the Ituri Forest. Emin had been deposed by his own officers in August 1887, and the Mahdists had overrun most of the province. After the Anglo-Egyptian force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-kitchener">Lord Kitchener</a> overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdist-state">Mahdist State</a> in 1898, Equatoria was administered by the British. Missionaries ran limited schools and clinics there.</p><p>On 18 August 1955 No. 2 Company of the Equatoria Corps, the southernmost unit of the Sudan Defence Force, mutinied at Torit rather than move north, and similar mutinies followed at Juba, Yei, Yambio and Maridi. This marked the beginning of the first civil war in southern Sudan, and the rebellion later became known as Anya Nya. The war ended with the Addis Ababa Agreement, signed on 27 March 1972, which gave the South autonomy in three provinces, Equatoria, Bahr el Ghazal and Upper Nile. Fighting resumed in 1983, and the second civil war lasted until 2004.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Equatoria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Khedivate of Egypt annexed Darfur in 1874 and <b>Equatoria</b>, and appointed Charles Gordon governor-general to suppress the slave trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/turco-egyptian-sudan">Turco-Egyptian Sudan</a></li></ul></section>
